##### 3. Code
###### 3.1 [*Change of Variables*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_of_variables)

In mathematics, a [*change of variables*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_of_variables) is a basic technique used to *simplify* problems in which the original variables are replaced with functions of other variables. The intent is that when expressed in *new* variables, the problem may become *simpler*, or equivalent to a better understood problem. 

Suppose a single *random* variable $X$ is changed by a *monotonic, differentiable* transformation $Y=g(X)$. Then:

$Y = g(X),$     
$X = g^{-1}(Y),$  
$X = g^{-1}(g(X))\;\text{ and }$

$Y = g(g^{-1}(Y)).$

Now a function $f(X)$ becomes a *transformed* function $\tilde f(Y)$ defined as

$\tilde f(Y) = f(g^{-1}(Y))\tag{cf. DLFC, (2.70)}.$

Its *derivative* is according to the *chain rule*:

$\tilde f'(Y) = f'(g^{-1}(Y))g'^{-1}(Y)$

and for *modes* $\hat Y, \hat X$ plugged into the derivative

$\tilde f'(\hat Y) = f'(g^{-1}(\hat Y))g'^{-1}(\hat Y)\tag{cf. DLFC, (2.72)}.$

If the functions $\tilde f, f$ were *monotonic, differentiable* but otherwise *unconstrained* functions we could derive the $modeY = mode(Y) = \hat Y$ by simple plugging $modeX = mode(X) = \hat X$ into the transformation function $g(X)$:

$\hat Y = g(\hat X).$

This is correct, because at *modes* $\hat X, \hat Y$ the derivatives should be zero

$f'(\hat X) = 0\;\text{ and }\tilde f'(\hat Y) = 0.$

Under the assumptions

$g'^{-1}(\hat Y) ≠ 0 \;\text{ and } f'(\hat X) = \tilde f'(\hat Y) = f'(g^{-1}(\hat Y)) = 0$

we can deduce

$\hat X = g^{-1}(\hat Y)\;\text{ and } \hat Y = g(\hat X).$

But this were *only* true if $f$ was an *unconstrained* function. This *not* true for probability density functions. They are *constrained* by both *nonnegative* values and *integrals* of measure 1.

"

