# ╔═╡ d7f0cc78-0e9d-497a-bfa3-3b4f423c532f
md"
---
###### 3.8 Transformation of *Densities* and the [*Jacobian Determinant*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_matrix_and_determinant)
A *change of variables* $Y = g(X)$ has a *different* effect whether a function $f(X)$ is a *density* or a common *monotonic* but otherwise *unconstrained* function. 

A density is *constrained* to *nonnegative* values (*constraint 1*) and its *integral* over the full range of the random variable has to be *one* (*constraint 2*). Thus the effect of a variable change on a density $f_X(x)$ *cannot* be calculated by the simple transformation

$f_Y(y) \cancel{:=} f_X(g^{-1}(y)) \tag{cf. DLFC, (2.70)}.$ 

*Probabilities* remain *unchanged* during [*change of variables*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_of_variables). This is a consequence of *constraint 2*. 

$f_Y(y)\cdot\delta y \simeq f_X(x)\cdot\delta x\;;\text{ with }\delta x > 0.$

Moving $δy$ to the right side of the equation and taking *absolute* values because of the *constraint 1* gives

$f_Y(y) \simeq f_X(x)\cdot\left|\frac{\delta x}{\delta y}\right|.$

Taking the limit

$\lim_{\delta x \to 0} \left(f_Y(y) \simeq f_X(x)\cdot\left|\frac{\delta x}{\delta y}\right|\right) \Rightarrow f_Y(y) = f_X(x)\left|\frac{dx}{dy}\right|$

$f_Y(y) = f_X(g^{-1}(y))\left|\frac{dg^{-1}(y)}{dy}\right|$ 
$f_Y(y) = f_X(g^{-1}(y))\left|(g^{-1})'(y)\right|\tag{cf. DLFC, (2.71)}.$

The expression $\left|\frac{dx}{dy}\right|$ is known as the [*Jacobian determinant*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_matrix_and_determinant). The *Jacobian* is *correction* factor, which scales the density *by the ratio of lengths* (Bishop & Bishop, 2024, p.42).

"

# ╔═╡ 9db11048-f743-4fd6-9374-d920df3e6398
# f_X(g^{-1}(y)) without correction factor
fX_gInv(ys) = pdf.(Normal(modeX, σX), gInv.(ys))  # density without correction factor

# ╔═╡ a679883f-c97c-41f4-8333-41b0b998099b
# f_Y(y) includng correction factor               # density incl. correction factor
fY(ys) = pdf.(Normal(modeX, σX), gInv.(ys)) .* abs.(1.0 ./(ys .- ys.^2))

# ╔═╡ 54f2f90b-ed2a-498c-9f23-a446ee403981
md"
---
###### 3.9 *Derivatives* of the *Logit* Function $(g^{-1})'(y)$ and Transformed Density $f'_Y(y)$

The symbolic *derivative* $(g^{-1})'(y)$ is

$(g^{-1})'(y) = \frac{dg^{-1}(y)}{dy} = \frac{d \ln\left(\frac{y}{1 - Y}\right) + 5}{dy} = \frac{1}{y-y^2}$

so that the *transformed* density $f_Y(y)$ is

$f_Y(y) = f_X(g^{-1}(y)) \cdot \left|\frac{1}{y-y^2}\right| =$
$=N\left(g^{-1}(y); μ_X=mode(X), \sigma_X=1\right)\cdot \left|\frac{1}{y-y^2}\right|.$

The Julia code including the *broadcasting* operator '$.$' is

$f_Y(y) := pdf.(Normal(modeX, σX), gInv.(ys)) .* abs.(1.0 ./(ys .- ys.^2)).$

We plot $f_Y(y)$ in its rudimentary form $f_{4Y}(y)$ *without* the *correction* factor $\left|\frac{1}{y-y^2}\right|$ (left, below) and in its complete form $f_Y(y)$ *including* it (right, below). We see clearly that only the *complete* form including the *correction factor* $\left|\frac{1}{y-y^2}\right|$ fits the *pmf* $p_Y$ perfectly.

"

